{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"Strike_Dummy","to":"Strike Dummy"},{"from":"Du-Vu_Doll","to":"Du-Vu Doll"},{"from":"Fusion_Hammer","to":"Fusion Hammer"},{"from":"Bronze_Scales","to":"Bronze Scales"},{"from":"Bird-Faced_Urn","to":"Bird-Faced Urn"},{"from":"Snecko_Skull","to":"Snecko Skull"},{"from":"Dream_Catcher","to":"Dream Catcher"},{"from":"Frozen_Egg","to":"Frozen Egg"},{"from":"Black_Star","to":"Black Star"},{"from":"Blue_Candle","to":"Blue Candle"},{"from":"Dolly's_Mirror","to":"Dolly's Mirror"},{"from":"Membership_Card","to":"Membership Card"},{"from":"Tiny_House","to":"Tiny House"},{"from":"Prayer_Wheel","to":"Prayer Wheel"}],"pages":{"3025":{"pageid":3025,"ns":0,"title":"Bird-Faced Urn","revisions":[{"revid":55648,"parentid":50871,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Bird-Faced Urn\n|Image       = BirdFacedUrn.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = Whenever you play a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} card, heal 2 HP.\n|Flavor      = This urn shows the crow god Mazaleth looking mischievous.\n}}\n{{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Creative AI}} can allow for a lot of healing if the player's deck is capable of staying in combat for many turns.\n** [[Defect]] can find themselves with a deck that can automatically {{KW|Block}} against non-scaling enemies by using {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, {{BD|Focus}}, and extra Orb slots (such as from {{C|Capacitor}}) to repeatedly, and safely, play the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} generated by {{C|Creative AI}} each turn until they can end combat with full HP.\n* {{R|Bottled Tornado}} (or any {{KW|Innate}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} like {{C|Infinite Blades}}+) can guarantee some healing from {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} at the start of every combat.\n* Double-playing {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} with {{C|Amplify}}, {{C|Echo Form}}, or {{P|Duplication Potion}} will trigger {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}'s healing twice.\n* Cards can only ever be played during combat, so {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} can always benefit from {{R|Magic Flower}}; healing 3 HP per {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} instead of 2 HP.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The god ''Mazaleth'' in {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}'s flavor text refers to the {{M|Awakened One}}.\n** {{M|Awakened One|Awakened One's}} {{BD|Curiosity}} also triggers when playing {{QueryLink|Cards|type:power|Power}} cards, just like the {{R|Bird-Faced Urn}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 53: Blizzard'''\n** Updated relic asset for Bird-Faced Urn.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Bird-Faced, Well-Laid, All-Out, Self-Forming gets hyphenated.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3073":{"pageid":3073,"ns":0,"title":"Black Star","revisions":[{"revid":55650,"parentid":50915,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Black Star\n|Image       = BlackStar.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Elites now drop an additional relic when defeated.\n|Flavor      = Originally discovered in the town of the serpent, aside a solitary candle.\n}}\n{{R|Black Star}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Black Star}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n* Unlike other \"[[Elite]] [[Relics]]\" (such as {{R|Preserved Insect}} or {{R|Sling of Courage}}), {{R|Black Star}} has no effect on the [[Elites]] in the {{E|Dead Adventurer}} and {{E|The Colosseum}} events. Their combat rewards will '''not''' drop an additional relic with {{R|Black Star}}.\n* The extra relic dropped by {{R|Black Star}} will never be {{R|Girya}}, {{R|Peace Pipe}}, or {{R|Shovel}}.\n** This is likely to prevent the player from accidentally acquiring too many of these \"[[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]] relics\" as the game normally imposes a cap of owning no more than 2 relics which add an option to [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]].\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Preserved Insect}} will make [[Elites]] easier to kill by reducing their HP by 25%.\n* {{R|Sling of Courage}} grants the player +2 {{BD|Strength}} in [[Elite]] combats, and can allow {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}-heavy decks to kill [[Elites]] more easily.\n* {{R|Slaver's Collar}} grants the player 1 additional {{KW|Energy}} in [[Elite]] combats, and can allow the player to kill [[Elites]] more easily.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* [[Potions]] will never drop from [[Elites#Super_Elites|Super Elites]] if the player has {{R|Black Star}}. The combat rewards screen can only hold a maximum of 5 items, and the Gold, card reward, [[Mechanics#Keys|Emerald Key]], and 2 [[Relics]] (a total of 5 items) prevent any [[Potions]] from being added to the rewards.\n** The community-made mod [https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1605833019 ''BaseMod''] has a setting to fix this behaviour by allowing more than 5 items to be added to the combat rewards.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"text-align:center\"\n! !! Modded !! Unmodded\n|-\n! Burning Elite\n| [[File:Burning_Elite_drops_a_Potion_with_Black_Star_(modded).png|200px]]<br>Drops a potion\n| [[File:Burning_Elite_does_not_drop_potion_with_Black_Star_(unmodded).png|200px]]<br>Blocked by 5-item max\n|-\n! Normal Elite\n| [[File:Elite_drops_a_Potion_with_Black_Star_(modded).png|200px]]<br>Drops a potion\n| [[File:Elite_drops_a_potion_with_Black_Star_(unmodded).png|200px]]<br>Drops a potion (fits within max)\n|}\n\n{{Clear}}\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The flavor text of {{R|Black Star}} is a reference to the David Bowie song ''Black Star''. One line of the song's lyrics is ''\"In the villa of \u00d8rmen, stands a solitary candle\"''. The word ''\u00d8rmen'' means ''Serpent'' in Norwegian, and there is a town in south-eastern Norway named [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%98rmen ''\u00d8rmen''] as well.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackstar_(song)</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Fixed issue where Black Star relic was always returning two relics of the same rarity.\n* '''Weekly Patch 48: Creative AI'''\n** Grammar: Aside -> Beside for Black Star's flavor text.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Black Star relic wording update to be more consistent with other relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** New boss relic: Black Star.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2400":{"pageid":2400,"ns":0,"title":"Blue Candle","revisions":[{"revid":56905,"parentid":55659,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Blue Candle\n|Image       = BlueCandle.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = {{KW|Unplayable}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} cards can now be played. <br>Whenever you play a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}, lose 1 HP and {{KW|Exhaust}} it.\n|Flavor      = The flame ignites when shrouded in darkness.\n|GivesToPlayer = Exhaust\n|InteractsWith = Unplayable\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Unplayable}}{{Keyword Infobox|Exhaust}}\n{{R|Blue Candle}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. This '''includes''' {{C|Pride}} which is normally playable, meaning the player will still lose 1 HP when playing it.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Blue Candle}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Blue Candle}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Blue Candle}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Blue Candle}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Blue Candle}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Blue Candle}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Blue Candle}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Blue Candle}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Blue Candle}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Blue Candle}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Blue Candle}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Because {{R|Blue Candle}} allows players to {{KW|Exhaust}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} for 0 {{KW|Energy}}, it has synergy with effects that activate upon {{KW|Exhaust}} such as:\n** {{R|Charon's Ashes}} deals 3 damage to all enemies.\n** {{R|Dead Branch}} creates a random card in hand.\n** {{C|Feel No Pain}} grants 3(4) {{KW|Block}}.\n** {{C|Dark Embrace}} draws 1 card.\n* Because playing {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} with {{R|Blue Candle}} causes the player to lose a minimal amount of HP, it has synergy with effects that activate when the player loses health such as:\n** {{R|Runic Cube}} draws 1 card.\n** {{C|Rupture}} grants 1(2) {{BD|Strength}}.\n* {{R|Tungsten Rod}} removes the HP cost to play {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}}.\n** This may be undesirable if the player has the aforementioned synergies with {{R|Runic Cube}} or {{C|Rupture}}.\n* Increments {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter after the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} is played.\n* There is a (very rare) infinite card draw combo that can be performed with {{R|Ink Bottle}}, {{C|Necronomicurse}}, {{R|Blue Candle}}, and {{R|Tungsten Rod}}.\n** The {{C|Necronomicurse}} can be repeatedly {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} with {{R|Blue Candle}} (which counts as +1 card played for {{R|Ink Bottle}}'s counter) and {{R|Tungsten Rod}} prevents any HP from being lost.\n** When combined with sufficient repeatable {{KW|Energy}} generation (such as enough copies of {{C|Concentrate}} or {{C|TURBO}}, or a small deck combined with {{R|Sundial}}), this can allow the player to infinitely draw and play every card in their deck until they win the combat.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* A new copy of {{C|Necronomicurse}} will be added to the player's hand if it is {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}}, and thus it effectively cannot be removed by {{R|Blue Candle}}.\n** However, this allows the player to repeatedly activate the above synergies.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Strange Spoon}} can cause the {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} to sometimes not {{KW|Exhaust}}, effectively costing the player 1 HP just to discard the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}.\n* {{C|Pride}} is already able to be played to {{KW|Exhaust}} it, so having {{R|Blue Candle}} will only cause the player to lose HP. (This may be considered an upside if the player wishes to take damage)\n\n== Trivia ==\n* References Blue Candle, an item from The Legend of Zelda.<ref>https://www.zeldadungeon.net/wiki/Candle#BS_The_Legend_of_Zelda</ref> (As confirmed by the developers; ''citation needed'')\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Fixed certain Status and Curses triggering their end of turn effects by playing them normally with Medical Kit or Blue Candle under specific circumstances.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** The keyword 'exhausts' now shows the Exhaust hover tip (Blue Candle, etc).\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Blue Candle and Medical Kit wording changed to better reflect how they work with cards like Slimed.\n* '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''\n** Fixed issue where Regret/Decay/Doubt/Burn fails to exhaust via Blue Candle and Medical Kit.\n* '''Weekly Patch 7: Setting Up All the Things'''\n** Blue Candle and Medical Kit should now work with Choke and Thousand Cuts.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Blue Candle relic's flavor text typo fix.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance'''\n** Strange Spoon affects cards exhausted by Medical Kit and Blue Candle now.\n** AfterImage now works when playing Curse/Status cards via Medical Kit and Blue Candle.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2510":{"pageid":2510,"ns":0,"title":"Bronze Scales","revisions":[{"revid":56915,"parentid":55669,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Bronze Scales\n|Image       = BronzeScales.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Start each combat with 3 {{BD|Thorns}}.\n|Flavor      = The sharp scales of the Guardian. Rearranges itself to protect its user.\n|GivesToPlayer = Thorns\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Thorns}}{{R|Bronze Scales}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Bronze Scales}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Bronze Scales}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Bronze Scales}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Bronze Scales}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Bronze Scales}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Bronze Scales}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Bronze Scales}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Bronze Scales}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Bronze Scales}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Bronze Scales}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* General synergy with {{KW|Block}} and other forms of damage mitigation (such as {{C|Piercing Wail}} or {{BD|Intangible}}) to deal chip damage to enemies even when not playing any damaging cards.\n* The {{BD|Thorns}} granted by {{R|Bronze Scales}} stacks with other forms of '''Thorns''' such as {{C|Caltrops}} or {{P|Liquid Bronze}}.\n* {{C|Flame Barrier}} provides both {{KW|Block}} and its own form of damage when hit by an enemy, overall increasing the effectiveness on turns with multiple enemy attacks.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Despite the flavor text mentioning that {{E|The Guardian}} is the source of the scales, '''The Guardian''' never actually uses {{BD|Thorns}}. It instead has {{BD|Sharp Hide}} which deals damage exactly once when an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} is played regardless of how many times the '''attack''' actually hits it, while '''Thorns''' deals damage every time an attack hits.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** Updated description on Bronze Scales to match Liquid Bronze and other similar relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Bronze Scales damage increased from 2 -> 3 to better match The Guardian.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2520":{"pageid":2520,"ns":0,"title":"Damaru","revisions":[{"revid":56956,"parentid":55721,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Damaru\n|Image       = damaru.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Character   = Watcher\n|Description = At the start of your turn, gain 1 {{BD|Mantra}}.\n|Flavor      = The sound of the small drum keeps your mind awake, revealing a path forward.\n|GivesToPlayer = Mantra\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Mantra}}\n{{R|Damaru}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Watcher|Watcher-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Damaru}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Damaru}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Damaru}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Damaru}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Damaru}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Damaru}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Damaru}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Damaru}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Damaru}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Damaru}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Watcher]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Damaru}} rarely generates enough {{BD|Mantra}} to enter {{KW|Divinity}} on its own. Pairing it with other {{BD|Mantra}}-generating cards like {{C|Prostrate}} and {{C|Devotion}} can allow the player to enter {{KW|Divinity}} more quickly than if they didn't possess {{R|Damaru}}, as even the slow trickle of additional {{BD|Mantra}} is enough to close the gap and reach the required amount of 10.\n* Increases the damage of {{C|Brilliance}} each turn without needing to draw or play any cards.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A damaru is a small two-headed drum often seen in Hindu and Buddhist cultures, and are typically used as sacred instruments. The drum shell is traditionally made with wood, and it resembles a \"yo-yo-like\" shape with a thinner middle circumference and a larger outer circumference on both ends. Leather is then stretched over the heads and fixed with metal rings. The drum is played by holding the thin middle section and quickly rocking the wrist back and forth so that strings with beads at the end of them strike the drum faces to produce their unique sound.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damaru</ref>\n* If the player selects {{R|Damaru}} from their relic bar (or in the compendium from the main menu), pressing the number keys can be used to play music. The pitch of the notes matches the number pressed, with low notes being played by smaller numbers and high notes played by larger numbers (and also 0).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Common Relic: Damaru\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2488":{"pageid":2488,"ns":0,"title":"Dolly's Mirror","revisions":[{"revid":50956,"parentid":37152,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Dolly's Mirror\n|Image       = DollysMirror.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = Upon pickup, obtain an additional copy of a card in your deck.\n|Flavor      = I look funny in this.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Dolly's Mirror}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. On pickup, it allows the player to choose any card in their deck to gain an exact duplicate of.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics and instead replaces them with random {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, or {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* When used on a card with dynamic scaling (such as {{C|Searing Blow}}, {{C|Genetic Algorithm}}, and {{C|Ritual Dagger}}), the duplicate will retain the original's current value. The two cards will scale themselves completely separately afterwards.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Cards that have been bottled by {{R|Bottled Flame}}, {{R|Bottled Lightning}}, or {{R|Bottled Tornado}} can still be duplicated from {{R|Dolly's Mirror}} as normal. However, the duplicated card will not be bottled and is not guaranteed to be in the player's opening hand.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The name {{R|Dolly's Mirror}} is a reference to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolly_(sheep) Dolly the sheep]; the first mammal that was successfully cloned from the cell of an adult in the same species.\n** Contrary to popular belief, Dolly was not the first mammal that was successfully cloned. The first mammal ever successfully cloned was a sheep in 1984 which was cloned from a sheep embryo by the University of Cambridge.<ref>https://web.archive.org/web/20211111151011/http://dolly.roslin.ed.ac.uk/facts/the-life-of-dolly/index.html</ref> Dolly was specifically the first mammal cloned ''from a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somatic_cell somatic cell] taken directly from an adult of a given species'' (specifically from the mammary gland of Dolly's surrogate mother).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding shop relic Dolly's Mirror.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2521":{"pageid":2521,"ns":0,"title":"Dream Catcher","revisions":[{"revid":50809,"parentid":37153,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Dream Catcher\n|Image       = DreamCatcher.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Whenever you Rest, you may add a card to your deck.\n|Flavor      = The northern tribes would often use dream catchers at night, believing they led to self improvement.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Dream Catcher}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|Common:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. Whenever the '''Rest''' option is selected at a [[Map Locations#Rest Site|Rest Site]], the player is presented with a card reward after the resting animation finishes. They have the option to skip and not take any of the presented cards as normal.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Dream Catcher}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Dream Catcher}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Dream Catcher}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Dream Catcher}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Dream Catcher}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Dream Catcher}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Dream Catcher}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Dream Catcher}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Dream Catcher}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Dream Catcher}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\nWhile the card reward from {{R|Dream Catcher}} does not present a reward screen for the player to choose to open the card reward (it immediately presents the card options to the player with no intermediate screen), it otherwise acts like a normal card reward.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Question Card}} and will present 4 card options to choose from.\n* {{R|Singing Bowl}} can be used to gain 2 Max HP while skipping the presented cards.\n* {{R|Molten Egg}}, {{R|Toxic Egg}}, and {{R|Frozen Egg}} will upgrade their relevant card types as expected.\n* {{R|Prismatic Shard}} will make it possible for cards of any color to be presented as expected.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Fusion Hammer}} disables the '''Smith''' option at [[Map Locations#Rest Site|Rest Sites]], so '''Resting''' is likely the only option that can be taken.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Busted Crown}} will only present 1 card option to choose from.\n** Just like with normal card rewards from combat, having both {{R|Busted Crown}} and {{R|Question Card}} will provide 2 cards to choose from.\n* {{R|Coffee Dripper}} disables the '''Rest''' option at [[Map Locations#Rest Site|Rest Sites]], which makes {{R|Dream Catcher}} functionally useless.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* In some Native American and First Nations cultures, a dream catcher (or dreamcatcher) is a handmade hoop, traditionally made of wood from a willow tree, which has a rope net woven into it. They are often further decorated with feathers, beads, and colored rope. They are traditionally hung above cradles or beds as a protective charm.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dreamcatcher</ref>\n** They were popularized and achieved widespread use outside of traditional Native American and First Nation cultures due to the Pan-Indian movement in the mid-to-late 1900s where they were popular \"Native craft items\".<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan-Indianism</ref>\n** In [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ojibwe_language Ojibwe], an indigenous language of North America of the Algonquian language family, they are called ''asabikeshiinh'', meaning \"spider\" (inanimate).\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Dream Catcher relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Dream Catcher relic no longer opens a rewards screen if there will be no cards.\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Dream Catcher's wording is made more consistent with Regal Pillow.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2371":{"pageid":2371,"ns":0,"title":"Du-Vu Doll","revisions":[{"revid":55752,"parentid":50875,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Du-Vu Doll\n|Image       = Du-VuDoll.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = For each {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} in your deck, start each combat with 1 {{BD|Strength}}.\n|Flavor      = A doll devised to gain strength from malicious energy.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}}{{R|Du-Vu Doll}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. Its description also shows the number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} you have in your deck.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nWhen calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}'s {{BD|Strength}} is an additive bonus.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} only checks the player's ''deck'' at the start of combat, so any changes during combat do not affect the {{BD|Strength}} provided. This means the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} can be {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} mid-combat without reducing the {{BD|Strength}} gained.\n** Lots of efficient card draw effects (such as {{C|Acrobatics}} or {{C|Skim}}) can help draw past the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} in the player's deck as well.\n** {{R|Blue Candle}} can be particularly effective at this, especially when combined with {{C|Dark Embrace}} to immediately replace the {{KW|Exhaust|Exhausted}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} with another card.\n* On {{Asc|10|Ascension 10+}}, the player starts the run with {{C|Ascender's Bane}} in their deck which cannot be removed. This ensures {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} will always provide at least 1 {{BD|Strength}} (making it at least on par with {{R|Vajra}}).\n* {{R|Cursed Key}} will give the player a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} every time they open a [[Chest]].\n* {{M|Writhing Mass}} can give the player a {{C|Parasite}} once per combat.\n\n==== Cards ====\n* {{BD|Strength}} is applied to each hit in an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}}, allowing multi-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} (such as {{C|Sword Boomerang}}, {{C|Flechettes}}, {{C|Barrage}}, and {{C|Bowling Bash}}) to benefit from {{BD|Strength}} multiple times.\n* {{C|Heavy Blade}} specifically benefits from {{BD|Strength}} multiple times in its effect, even though it's a single-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}}.\n* {{C|Reaper}}'s healing increases based on its unblocked damage, and therefore more {{BD|Strength}} will cause it to heal more HP.\n* {{C|Wallop}}'s {{KW|Block}} gain increases based on its unblocked damage, and therefore more {{BD|Strength}} will cause it to gain more {{KW|Block}}.\n* {{C|Limit Break}} can be used to multiply the player's current {{BD|Strength}}, although it won't be a very significant increase if their only {{BD|Strength}} source is {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} and they don't have very many {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Omamori}} will prevent the next 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} from being added to the player's deck, preventing {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} from granting more {{BD|Strength}}.\n* If a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} is removed from the player's deck, {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} will subsequently provide 1 less {{BD|Strength}} at the start of combat.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} is a reference to ''Voodoo Dolls''; a type of effigy used in magical traditions in many cultures around the world. Pins are inserted into the doll to supposedly create magical effects.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voodoo_doll</ref>\n** Despite its name, ''Voodoo Dolls'' have no relation to the cultural and religious traditions of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haitian_Vodou Haitian Vodou] or [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louisiana_Voodoo Louisiana Voodoo]. Such relations are only founded in pop culture and entertainment media which link the concept of ''Voodoo'' to black magic and malicious witchcraft.\n* It is not known why the relic's name is spelled ''Du-Vu'' to phonetically reverse the syllables of ''Voodoo''.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2317":{"pageid":2317,"ns":0,"title":"Ectoplasm","revisions":[{"revid":55420,"parentid":50922,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ectoplasm\n|Image       = Ectoplasm.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You can no longer gain [[Gold]].\n|Flavor      = This blob of slime and energy seems to pulse with life.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Ectoplasm}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]] at the end of Act 1, {{R|Ectoplasm}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 18 (the first floor of [[Act 2]]). This means {{R|Ectoplasm}} can't be obtained from the Boss Chest at the end of [[Act 2]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Ectoplasm}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* Price-reduction [[Relics]] (such as {{R|Smiling Mask}}, {{R|The Courier}}, and {{R|Membership Card}}) can help the player make more effective use of their remaining [[Gold]].\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n[[Gold]] gained from '''ANY''' source is prevented from being collected by the player while they have {{R|Ectoplasm}}. This includes:\n\n==== Map Locations ====\n* [[Gold]] rewarded from defeating [[Slay the Spire Wiki/Enemies|Enemies]], [[Elites]], and [[Bosses]].\n** {{M|Looter}} and {{M|Mugger}} will permanently steal some of the player's [[Gold]] because it cannot be re-collected after killing them.\n* The [[Gold]] that can sometimes be found in [[Chests]].\n\n==== Cards ====\n* {{C|Hand of Greed}}\n* {{C|Wish}} (via {{C|Fame and Fortune}})\n\n==== Relics ====\n* {{R|Ceramic Fish}}\n* {{R|Maw Bank}}\n* {{R|Old Coin}}\n* {{R|Tiny House}}\n* {{R|Bloody Idol}} (does not heal HP)\n* {{R|Golden Idol}}\n* {{R|Ssserpent Head}}\n\n==== Events ====\n* {{E|Golden Shrine}}\n* {{E|Wheel of Change}} (if the wheel lands on the [[Gold]] option)\n* {{E|Dead Adventurer}} (prevents both the [[Gold]] from {{Frame|Search}} or from defeating the [[Elite]] as normal)\n* {{E|Golden Idol}} (no direct [[Gold]] gain, but {{R|Golden Idol}} is rendered useless)\n* {{E|The Ssssserpent}}\n* {{E|Wing Statue}} (specifically the {{Frame|Destroy}} option)\n* {{E|World of Goop}} (forces the player to either permanently lose some [[Gold]] with the {{Frame|Leave It}} option, or keep their [[Gold]] and take damage for no additional benefit with the {{Frame|Gather Gold}} option)\n* {{E|Knowing Skull}} (specifically the {{Frame|Riches?}} option)\n* {{E|The Joust}} (forces the player to permanently lose 50 [[Gold]] and gain nothing even if they win the bet)\n* {{E|The Nest}} (specifically the {{Frame|Smash and Grab}} option)\n* {{E|Mind Bloom}} (specifically the {{Frame|I am Rich}} option)\n* {{E|The Moai Head}} (specifically the {{Frame|Offer Golden Idol}} option)\n* {{E|Tomb of Lord Red Mask}} (specifically the {{Frame|Don the Red Mask}} option)\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Ectoplasm (or simply \"ecto\") is a substance or spiritual energy created by supernatural forces or entities in media and pop culture.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ectoplasm_(paranormal)</ref>\n** The goop-like appearance is typically associated with ghosts, and was popularized by the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostbusters Ghost Busters] franchise. The visual design of {{R|Ectoplasm}} resembles this interpretation of ectoplasm.\n* The word \"ectoplasm\" comes from the Ancient Greek words ''ektos'' (meaning \"outside\") and ''plasma'' (meaning \"something formed\").\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2562":{"pageid":2562,"ns":0,"title":"Frozen Egg","revisions":[{"revid":55435,"parentid":50842,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Frozen Egg\n|Image       = FrozenEgg2.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Whenever you add a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} card to your deck, {{KW|Upgrade}} it.\n|Flavor      = The egg lies inert and frozen, never to hatch.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Upgrade}}{{R|Frozen Egg}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Frozen Egg}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Frozen Egg}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Frozen Egg}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Frozen Egg}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Frozen Egg}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Frozen Egg}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Frozen Egg}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Frozen Egg}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Frozen Egg}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Frozen Egg}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Frozen Egg}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Prayer Wheel}} and {{R|Question Card}} can provide more opportunities for the player to encounter {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} from card rewards.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} created mid-combat (such as from {{R|Nilry's Codex}} which adds to the draw pile, or {{C|White Noise}} which adds to the hand) will not be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} as {{R|Frozen Egg}} only '''Upgrades''' card that are added to the player's '''deck'''.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* In some niche scenarios, the player may prefer if a certain {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} were ''not'' upgraded, and {{R|Frozen Egg}} does not give the player a choice on whether the cards be '''Upgraded''' or not. Some examples include:\n** Preferring a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} were ''not'' made {{KW|Innate}} from its {{KW|Upgrade}} (such as {{C|Infinite Blades}}, {{C|Hello World}}, or {{C|Battle Hymn}})\n** If the player has {{R|Snecko Eye}} and a way to upgrade cards mid-combat (such as {{C|Armaments}}), they will not be able to take advantage of reducing a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power's}} {{KW|Energy}} cost if {{R|Snecko Eye}} makes it more expensive than its base cost.\n*** For example, if {{R|Snecko Eye}} makes an un-{{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{C|Tools of the Trade}} cost 2 or 3 {{KW|Energy}}, '''Upgrading''' it will set its cost to 0 (the normal cost of {{C|Tools of the Trade}}+). But if it's already '''Upgraded''' in the player's deck from {{R|Frozen Egg}}, this trick cannot be performed.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Frozen Egg}} is one of the relics in the \"egg relic set\" which {{KW|Upgrade}} cards of different types when added to the player's deck; the others being {{R|Molten Egg}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} and {{R|Toxic Egg}} for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}}.\n* {{R|Frozen Egg}} is called <code>Frozen_Egg_2</code> internally as the egg relics were reworked in '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''. These reworked versions ended up being the final version of the egg relics, so their modified internal name became their default internal name.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Egg relics now update card rewards in reward screens upon pickup.\n** Egg relics update card previews on Pandora's Box confirmation screen.\n** Fixed egg relics not upgrading card received from A Note To Yourself.\n* '''Weekly Patch 53: Blizzard'''\n** Fix for Egg relics causing an issue with cards in the Library event screen.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Egg relics no longer spawn beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Gremlin Match and Keep event now has cards upgraded correctly.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Buying an egg relic in the shop will now correctly show cards in the shop upgraded.\n* '''Weekly Patch 31: Custom Mode'''\n** Match and Keep cards are now viewed as upgraded w/ Egg relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** Egg relics now show pre-upgraded cards for Sensory Stone event's choices.\n* '''Weekly Patch 24: Potions?!'''\n** The three egg relics will now show the cards they are affecting as upgraded in card reward screens and shops.\n* '''Weekly Patch 13: Thinking Ahead'''\n** Egg relics (Toxic, Molten, Frozen) can now appear at Neow and some other scenarios.\n* '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''\n** All 3 Egg relics (Molten/Toxic/Frozen) are now reworked. New eggs are Uncommon.\n** Frozen Egg: Whenever you add a Power card to your deck, it is Upgraded.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** You can no longer cancel out of the selection screen for Egg relics.\n** Frozen Egg is now Common (was Uncommon)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3079":{"pageid":3079,"ns":0,"title":"Fusion Hammer","revisions":[{"revid":50924,"parentid":37167,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Fusion Hammer\n|Image       = FusionHammer.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You can no longer Smith at Rest Sites.\n|Flavor      = Once wielded, the owner can never let go.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{Keyword Infobox|Upgrade}}{{R|Fusion Hammer}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Fusion Hammer}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Fusion Hammer}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* The other \"Rest Site Relics\", {{R|Girya}}, {{R|Peace Pipe}}, and {{R|Shovel}}, can provide the player with more options at [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]] to help them use these floors effectively when they're unable to Smith.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Fusion Hammer}} removes the player's ability to consistently {{KW|Upgrade}} important cards, as they can no longer rely on pathing to a [[Map Locations#Rest Site|Rest Site]] to gurantee an {{KW|Upgrade}} by Smithing.\n* If the player picks up a card with a high-value {{KW|Upgrade}} (such as {{C|Feed}}, {{C|Wraith Form}}, {{C|Hologram}}, or {{C|Talk to the Hand}}), they will not be able to guarantee that it can be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}}.\n\n<!-- Is this too much for the Fusion Hammer page? I'm wondering if it makes sense to move this exhaustive list to the Upgrade page and instead highlight only a few key Upgrade effects (maybe just Armaments, Apotheosis, Lesson Learned, Forge Potion, Eggs, and Warped Tongs?)-->\n=== Managing the Drawback ===\nThe drawback of {{R|Fusion Hammer}} can be lessened by finding alternative ways of {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrading}} cards in the player's deck. These include:\n\n==== Cards ====\n* {{C|Armaments}} (especially if it can be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} into {{C|Armaments}}+ before acquiring {{R|Fusion Hammer}} or through other means)\n* {{C|Lesson Learned}}\n* {{C|Master Reality}}\n* {{C|Apotheosis}} (especially if bottled with {{R|Bottled Lightning}} or similar effects to consistently play it early in each combat)\n\n==== Relics ====\n* {{R|War Paint}} (only {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} at random)\n* {{R|Whetstone}} (only {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attacks|Attacks}} at random)\n* {{R|Molten Egg}}\n* {{R|Toxic Egg}}\n* {{R|Frozen Egg}}\n* {{R|Astrolabe}} (only {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} cards after they've been {{KW|Transform|Transformed}}, so it can't be used to {{KW|Upgrade}} specific cards in the player's deck)\n* {{R|Tiny House}} (only upgrades 1 card at random)\n* {{R|Warped Tongs}}\n\n==== Potions ====\n* {{P|Blessing of the Forge}}\n\n==== Events ====\n* {{E|Ominous Forge}}\n* {{E|Upgrade Shrine}}\n* {{E|Living Wall}} (specifically the {{Frame|Grow}} option)\n* {{E|Shining Light}} (only {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} 2 cards at random)\n* {{E|Ancient Writing}} (only {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Strike|starter Strikes}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Defend|starter Defends}} with the {{Frame|Simplicity}} option)\n* {{E|Designer In-Spire}} (specifically the {{Frame|Adjustments}} or {{Frame|Full Service}} options)\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 56: The Collector'''\n** Smith option now becomes disabled rather then disappearing when using Midas mod or Fusion Hammer relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** Adding boss relic Fusion Hammer.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3148":{"pageid":3148,"ns":0,"title":"Membership Card","revisions":[{"revid":50965,"parentid":37202,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Membership Card\n|Image       = MembershipCard.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = 50% discount on all products!\n|Flavor      = \"Bonus membership offer for my most valuable customers!\"\n}}\n{{R|Membership Card}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. It reduces the price of all items in the [[Shop]] by 50%, rounded to the nearest whole number (and rounding up on results ending in <code>.5</code>)<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rounding#Rounding_half_up</ref>.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n* For prices which are an odd number before purchasing {{R|Membership Card}}, the new price will round up after {{R|Membership Card}} is acquired (<code>101 * 0.5 = 50.5</code> which then rounds up to cost 51 [[Gold]] instead of the expected 50)\n* {{R|Membership Card}} immediately applies its discount to ALL [[Shop|Shops]] on purchase, meaning the rest of the items in the same [[Shop]] that it's purchased in will benefit from its price reduction.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|The Courier}}'s price reduction stacks with {{R|Membership Card}}; totalling a 60% reduction in the [[Merchant]]'s prices.\n* {{R|Membership Card}} reduces the price of ''everything'' in the [[Shop]], including the price of the card removal service.\n** This also means the cost of the card removal service will only increase by 12.5 [[Gold]] each time it's used (rounded up).\n\n==== Impact on Gold Gain ====\nEffects that grant [[Gold]] may be thought of as ''\"granting twice as much gold\"'' since all items in the [[Shop]] now cost half as much.\n\n===== Map Locations =====\n* [[Monsters|Normal encounters]] effectively drop 20-40 [[Gold]].\n* [[Elites]] effectively drop 50-70 [[Gold]].\n* [[Bosses]] effectively drop 190-210 ({{Asc|13|142-158}}) [[Gold]].\n* [[Gold#Chests|Small Chests]] have a 50% chance to effectively drop 46-56 [[Gold]].\n* [[Gold#Chests|Medium Chests]] have a 35% chance to effectively drop 90-110 [[Gold]].\n* [[Gold#Chests|Large Chests]] have a 50% chance to effectively drop 136-166 [[Gold]].\n\n===== Cards =====\n* {{C|Hand of Greed}} effectively gives 40 (50) [[Gold]] on {{KW|Fatal}}.\n* {{C|Wish}} (via {{C|Fame and Fortune}}) effectively gives 50 (60) [[Gold]] when played.\n\n===== Relics =====\n* {{R|Ceramic Fish}} effectively gives 18 [[Gold]] per card added to the player's deck.\n* {{R|Maw Bank}} effectively gives 24 [[Gold]] per floor ascended until it breaks.\n* {{R|Old Coin}} effectively gives 600 [[Gold]] on pickup.\n* {{R|Tiny House}} effectively gives 100 [[Gold]] on pickup.\n* {{R|Golden Idol}} effectively gives a 50% increase in [[Gold]] gain from combat.\n* {{R|Ssserpent Head}} effectively gives 100 [[Gold]] per [[Map_Locations#Unknown|? Room]] entered.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The price reduction of {{R|Membership Card}} can help player make more effective use of their limited [[Gold]] if they have {{R|Ectoplasm}}, although it will be less beneficial compared to if the player could continue to gain [[Gold]] if they didn't have {{R|Ectoplasm}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The end of combat tip ''\"Percentage-based calculations always round down at the end.\"'' is technically not true, as this is a percentage-based calculation that doesn't always round down.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Membership Card relic nerf: Only applies a discount now. (used to increase chance of shops appearing)\n** Saving and Loading with Membership Card relic no longer generates a new map.\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Membership Card relic's discount buffed.\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Membership Card relic no longer incorrectly lowers ? room chance.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2525":{"pageid":2525,"ns":0,"title":"Nunchaku","revisions":[{"revid":50815,"parentid":37214,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Nunchaku\n|Image       = Nunchaku.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Every time you play 10 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, gain 1 {{KW|Energy}}.\n|Flavor      = A good training tool. Improves the posture and agility of the wielder.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Nunchaku}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. The counter that keeps track of how many {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} have been played is not reset between turns or combats.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Nunchaku}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Nunchaku}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Nunchaku}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Nunchaku}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Nunchaku}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Nunchaku}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Nunchaku}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Nunchaku}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Nunchaku}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Nunchaku}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Ninja Scroll}} allows for an easy +3 to {{R|Nunchaku}}'s counter at the start of every combat.\n* Cards which generate multiple cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} (such as {{C|Blade Dance}} and {{C|Storm of Steel}}) can contribute a lot of progress to {{R|Nunchaku}}'s counter.\n* {{R|Ice Cream}} can preserve unused {{KW|Energy}} from the bonus for later turns.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Nunchaku}} is one of the relics in the \"ninja relics set\" which are all tools used in East-Asian Martial Arts and all synergize with a certain number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} played; the others being {{R|Shuriken}}, {{R|Kunai}}, {{R|Ornamental Fan}}, and {{R|Ninja Scroll}}.\n** Honorable mention to {{R|Pen Nib}} which fits the ''mechanical'' theme of the ninja relics, but has no actual ''visual'' theming with ninjas or East-Asian Martial Arts.\n* Nunchaku are traditional East-Asian martial arts weapons consisting of two sticks connected to each other by a short metal chain or rope.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nunchaku</ref>\n** In Western society, they are often referred to as \"nunchuks\" or \"nunchucks\".\n* The flavor text ''\"A good training tool. Improves the posture and agility of the wielder.\"'' is accurate to the primary real-world use of nunchaku. They are traditionally used as a training weapon to improve the user's development of quick hand movements and posture.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''10-24-2019'''\n** Nunchaku relic is now Common.\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Adding rare relic Nunchaku.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1530":{"pageid":1530,"ns":0,"title":"Omamori","revisions":[{"revid":55473,"parentid":50817,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Omamori\n|Image       = Omamori.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Negate the next 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} you obtain.\n|Flavor      = A common charm for staving off vile spirits. This one seems to possess a spark of divine energy.\n}}\n{{R|Omamori}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Omamori}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Omamori}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Omamori}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Omamori}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Omamori}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Omamori}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Omamori}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Omamori}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Omamori}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Omamori}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n* After {{R|Omamori}} has prevented 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}}, its icon will turn gray to indicate that all its charges have been used.\n* [[Transform|Transforming]] a card effectively removes it from the deck and then adds a new random card of the same type. Therefore, attempting to '''transform''' a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|curse}} into another one will trigger {{R|Omamori}} and effectively remove the original '''curse''' from the deck.\n\n=== Order ===\n* If {{R|Omamori}} is acquired as a reward from {{R|Calling Bell}} then it can never block {{C|Curse of the Bell}} since {{R|Calling Bell}} drops the relics ''after'' {{C|Curse of the Bell}} is added to the player's deck\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* [[Chests]] can be safely opened with {{R|Cursed Key}}.\n* The {{C|Necronomicurse}} that can be added to the player's deck by getting the {{R|Necronomicon}} from the {{E|Cursed Tome}} [[event]] will be blocked by {{R|Omamori}} even though the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|curse}} reads ''\"There is no escape from this {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}.\"''.\n* The {{C|Curse of the Bell}} added to the player's deck by acquiring {{R|Calling Bell}} will be blocked by {{R|Omamori}}.\n* Many [[Events]] provide powerful benefits in exchange for receiving {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|curses}}, or have multiple punishing choices where one choice involves taking a '''curse'''. {{R|Omamori}} can be used to ignore these drawbacks.\n* [[Transform]] effects (such as {{R|Astrolabe}} and some [[Events]]) can be used to effectively remove {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|curses}} that were originally acquired before {{R|Omamori}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} blocked by {{R|Omamori}} will not trigger the Max HP gain from {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}.\n* Prevents adding {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|curses}} in the player's deck to be used by {{C|Fire Breathing}} or {{R|Du-Vu Doll}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Omamori are Japanese paper or wooden amulets commonly sold at Shinto shrines and Buddhist temples. They are said to provide various forms of luck and protection.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omamori</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Omamori relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2535":{"pageid":2535,"ns":0,"title":"Pear","revisions":[{"revid":50855,"parentid":37228,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Pear\n|Image       = Pear.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Upon pickup, raise your Max HP by 10.\n|Flavor      = A common fruit before the Spireblight.\n}}\n{{R|Pear}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Pear}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Pear}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Pear}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Pear}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Pear}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Pear}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Pear}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Pear}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Pear}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Pear}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Pear}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* If {{R|Pear}} is removed by {{E|N'loth}}, the player's Max HP will not decrease.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Pear}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} rarity relic in the \"fruit relic set\" which all increase Max HP on pickup; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} {{R|Strawberry}} and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} {{R|Mango}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** HP-gain relics (Fruit & Waffle) now have consistent pickup wording.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1531":{"pageid":1531,"ns":0,"title":"Prayer Wheel","revisions":[{"revid":50890,"parentid":37233,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Prayer Wheel\n|Image       = PrayerWheel.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = Normal enemies drop an additional card reward.\n|Flavor      = The wheel continues to spin, never stopping.\n}}\n{{R|Prayer Wheel}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It makes [[Monsters|''normal'' enemies]] offer an additional card reward in the end of combat rewards. This effect does '''not''' apply to [[Elite]] combat rewards or the guaranteed {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare card}} reward after defeating a [[Boss]]. The extra card reward is otherwise functionally identical to a \"normal\" card reward.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Prayer Wheel}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Prayer Wheel}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Prayer Wheel}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Prayer Wheel}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Prayer Wheel}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Prayer Wheel}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Prayer Wheel}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Prayer Wheel}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Prayer Wheel}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Prayer Wheel}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Prayer Wheel}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Prayer Wheel}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Question Card}} will also improve the card reward offered by {{R|Prayer Wheel}}. With both of these relics, a total of 8 cards are shown to the player after defeating [[Map Locations#Enemies|normal enemies]] (2 card rewards each showing 4 cards).\n* The extra card reward interacts with {{R|Singing Bowl}} normally, and can be used to gain 2 Max HP if the player chooses to. The player can theoretically gain 4 Max HP after winning a normal fight by skipping both card rewards in this way.\n* Potentially gain more gold from {{R|Ceramic Fish}} since approximately twice the amount of card rewards are offered.\n* Generally improves the number of {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} cards offered thanks to the additional cards seen will increase the [[Cards#Card_Distribution|rare card chance]] more quickly than usual.\n** Further improved by {{R|N'loth's Gift}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Lessens the drawback from {{R|Busted Crown}}.\n** The player effectively went from seeing 1 card after combat (from the debuffed card reward thanks to '''Busted Crown''') to now seeing 2 total cards (2 card rewards that each show 1 card).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A prayer wheel is a kind of rotating cylindrical wheel used in Buddhism for prayer and mantra recitation.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prayer_wheel</ref> In its simplest form, a prayer wheel is a wooden or metal cylinder that has a prayer or mantra written on the outside, which rotates around an axle.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Prayer Wheel relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Prayer Wheel relic reworked and is now rare. (was common)\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Prayer Wheel now says that chests also contain cards rather than 'contains cards' to avoid confusion.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2373":{"pageid":2373,"ns":0,"title":"Shuriken","revisions":[{"revid":55939,"parentid":50857,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}} \n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Shuriken\n|Image       = Shuriken.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Every time you play 3 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} in a single turn, gain 1 {{BD|Strength}}.\n|Flavor      = Lightweight throwing weapons. Recommend going for the eyes.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}}\n{{R|Shuriken}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Shuriken}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Shuriken}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Shuriken}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Shuriken}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Shuriken}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Shuriken}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Shuriken}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Shuriken}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Shuriken}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Shuriken}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Shuriken}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* Works especially well with decks that generate many low-cost or zero-cost {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}:\n** [[Ironclad]] can combine {{C|Anger}}, {{C|Twin Strike}}, and {{C|Whirlwind}} for multiple {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} per turn.\n** [[Silent]]'s {{C|Shiv}}-generating cards like {{C|Blade Dance}}, {{C|Cloak and Dagger}}, and {{C|Infinite Blades}} allow frequent triggering of {{R|Shuriken}}.\n** [[Defect]] can utilize {{C|Claw}} and other cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} for repeated activations.\n** [[Watcher]] can use {{C|Flurry of Blows}} and other stance-based {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} to reliably trigger it.\n* Works well with the other \"ninja relics\" which all interact with the number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} played in some way:\n** {{R|Kunai}} grants {{BD|Dexterity}} under similar conditions, complementing {{R|Shuriken}}'s {{BD|Strength}}.\n** {{R|Nunchaku}} provides extra energy every 10 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, supporting attack-heavy decks.\n** {{R|Ornamental Fan}} provides {{KW|Block}} for each trigger.\n** {{R|Ninja Scroll}} allows for an easy trigger at the start of every combat.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* Decks that rely heavily on {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} or {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} (e.g. block-focused [[Defect]] with {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, or [[Silent]] {{BD|Poison}} decks without {{C|Shiv|Shivs}}) may struggle to activate {{R|Shuriken}} consistently.\n* When combined with effects that reduce card draw or limit playable cards (e.g. {{M|Time Eater}} or {{R|Velvet Choker}}), {{R|Shuriken}}'s usefulness is diminished.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Shuriken}} is one of the relics in the \"ninja relics set\" which are all tools used in East-Asian Martial Arts and all synergize with a certain number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} played; the others being {{R|Kunai}}, {{R|Nunchaku}}, {{R|Ornamental Fan}}, and {{R|Ninja Scroll}}.\n** Honorable mention to {{R|Pen Nib}} which fits the ''mechanical'' theme of the ninja relics, but has no actual ''visual'' theming with ninjas or East-Asian Martial Arts.\n* Shuriken (also known as ''throwing stars'' or ''ninja stars'') are a traditional Japanese weapon used by samurai or ninja. They are highly versatile and can be used in a number of ways, such as close-range weapons like a makeshift dagger, as short-range thrown weapons, as a distraction tool by throwing away from the user and loudly hitting an object farther away, or as makeshift caltrops by driving them into the ground.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuriken</ref>\n** There are many different types and form-factors of shuriken used throughout history. {{R|Shuriken}} is specifically a 4-point Hira-shuriken.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1800":{"pageid":1800,"ns":0,"title":"Snecko Skull","revisions":[{"revid":56366,"parentid":55949,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Snecko Skull\n| Image       = SnakeSkull.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Character   = Silent\n| Description = Whenever you apply {{BD|Poison}}, apply an additional 1 {{BD|Poison}}.\n| Flavor      = A snecko skull in pristine condition. Mysteriously clean and smooth, dirt and grime fall off inexplicably.\n| GivesToEnemy = Poison\n| InteractsWith = Poison\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Poison}}\n{{R|Snecko Skull}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Silent|Silent-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Snecko Skull}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Snecko Skull}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Snecko Skull}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Snecko Skull}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Snecko Skull}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Snecko Skull}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Snecko Skull}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Snecko Skull}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Snecko Skull}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Snecko Skull}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Silent]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Noxious Fumes}} will apply 3 (4) {{BD|Poison}} at the start of each turn.\n* {{C|Bouncing Flask}} will apply an additional {{BD|Poison}} on each bounce, applying an additional 3 (4) total.\n* {{C|Envenom}} will apply an additional {{BD|Poison}} each attack, functionally doubling the poison applied.\n* {{C|Catalyst}} will apply an additional {{BD|Poison}} after the doubling (tripling), slightly increasing the impact of {{C|Burst}}.\n* {{R|The Specimen}} will transfer 1 more {{BD|Poison}} than the defeated enemy had.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Because multiple copies of {{C|Noxious Fumes}} stack, only 1 additional {{BD|Poison}} will be applied regardless of how many copies are played. The same is true for {{C|Envenom}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Despite {{R|Snecko Skull}} synergising with {{BD|Poison}}, the {{M|Snecko}} enemy's {{Int|Snecko|Bite}} does not apply {{BD|Poison}} to the player. Even its {{BD|Vulnerable}} (and {{BD|Weak}}{{Asc|17}}) come from a {{Int|Snecko|Tail Whip}} attack and not its bite.\n** During normal gameplay, it is actually impossible for the player to have {{BD|Poison}} applied to themselves as no cards self-inflict {{BD|Poison}} and no enemy has an attack that afflicts {{BD|Poison}} on the player.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2406":{"pageid":2406,"ns":0,"title":"Strike Dummy","revisions":[{"revid":47652,"parentid":37266,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}} {{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Strike Dummy\n|Image       = StrikeDummy.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Cards containing \"Strike\" deal 3 additional damage.\n|Flavor      = It's beat up.\n}}\n{{R|Strike Dummy}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. Any card that contains the word \"Strike\" in its name deals an additional 3 damage. See [[#Interactions]] for a list of cards this applies to.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Strike Dummy}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Strike Dummy}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Strike Dummy}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Strike Dummy}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Strike Dummy}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Strike Dummy}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Strike Dummy}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Strike Dummy}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Strike Dummy}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Strike Dummy}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Strike Dummy}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n{{R|Strike Dummy}} improves any {{QueryLink|Cards|search:Strike|card with \"Strike\" in its name}}. The [[Silent]] and [[Ironclad]] have 5 Strike cards in their starting decks. The [[Watcher]] and [[Defect]] both have 4 Strike cards in their starting decks.\n\n=== Ironclad ===\nThe [[Ironclad]] has the most cards with 'Strike' in its name. These are:\n\n* {{C|Strike (Ironclad)|Strike}}\n* {{C|Perfected Strike}} also checks for cards with 'Strike' in their names.\n* {{C|Pommel Strike}}\n* {{C|Twin Strike}} benefits from {{R|Strike Dummy}} twice.\n* {{C|Wild Strike}}\n\n=== Silent ===\n* {{C|Strike (Silent)|Strike}}\n* {{C|Sneaky Strike}}\n\n=== Defect ===\n* {{C|Strike (Defect)|Strike}}\n* {{C|Meteor Strike}}\n* {{C|Thunder Strike}} is a notable interaction as it can hit multiple times, benefiting from {{R|Strike Dummy}} multiple times.\n\n=== Watcher ===\n* {{C|Strike (Watcher)|Strike}}\n* {{C|Windmill Strike}}\n\n=== Colorless ===\n* {{C|Swift Strike}}\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Strike Dummy}} is reminiscent of a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mu_ren_zhuang ''Mu Ren Zhuang''] (\"Wooden Man Post\", more often translated as \"The Wooden Dummy\" internationally). It is a training tool used in many disciplines of martial arts that aims to loosely replicate the body of a human in various positions. Trainees are encouraged to imagine the dummy as a body with arms and legs to practice striking an opponent based on the positioning of their limbs.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Added to the game\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3087":{"pageid":3087,"ns":0,"title":"Tiny House","revisions":[{"revid":50936,"parentid":37277,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Tiny House\n|Image       = TinyHouse.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Upon pickup, obtain 1 [[Potions|Potion]]. <br>Gain 50 [[Gold]]. <br>Raise your Max HP by 5. <br>Obtain 1 card. <br>{{KW|Upgrade}} 1 random card.\n|Flavor      = \"A near perfect implementation of miniaturization. My finest work to date, but still not adequate.\" - The Architect\n}}\n{{R|Tiny House}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. The \"obtain 1 card\" effect acts as a normal card reward and will show the player 3 cards of which they can choose 1 to permanently add to their deck.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Tiny House}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* The card reward can be skipped with {{R|Singing Bowl}} to gain +2 Max HP.\n* The random {{KW|Upgrade}} may be able to help manage the player's lack of {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} if they have {{R|Fusion Hammer}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The card reward is affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}}.\n* Because the player is offered the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} reward ''after'' the guaranteed {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare card}} from defeating a [[Boss]], the card reward is guaranteed to '''not''' have any {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare cards}} in it* . For more information on rare card chance, see [[Cards#Card_Distribution|Card Distribution]].\n** * It is technically possible for {{R|Tiny House}} to have a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare card}} in it due to a special case with [[Cards#Card_Distribution|rare card chance]] when getting {{R|Tiny House}} from Neow's [[Boss Swap]]. But when acquired normally from a [[Boss]], it is impossible for this to occur.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Busted Crown}} will reduce the cards shown in the card reward by 2.\n* {{R|Sozu}} will prevent the [[Potion]] from being acquired.\n* {{R|Ectoplasm}} will prevent the [[Gold]] from being acquired.\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Tiny House relic buff: 30 -> 50 gold.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Tiny House relic wording improved.\n* '''Weekly Patch 12: Lunar New Year'''\n** Tiny House relic gets a buff. Now upgrades 1 random card as well.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}